course: dictionary
lesson: 13333
title: Gunboats on the river
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A gunboat is a small warship with big guns. It is built to move on rivers and near the coast, where bigger ships may not fit. «The gunboat sailed slowly up the river.» The word gun tells you it carries weapons, and boat tells you it sails on water.
explain: In the 1800s, the British Empire sent gunboats to patrol rivers in China and Africa. «The crew watched the banks from the deck of the gunboat.» Patrol means to travel around an area to keep watch over it.
explain: The phrase gunboat diplomacy is different. «The strong country sent a gunboat to make the weaker country sign the deal.» Diplomacy means dealing with other countries through talks and agreements. Gunboat diplomacy means a powerful country forcing weaker ones to agree by threatening to use its navy or army.
fact: A gunboat is a small warship with big guns.
fact: Gunboats were often used on rivers and near the coast.
fact: In the 1800s, gunboats patrolled rivers in China and Africa.
fact: «Gunboat diplomacy» means a strong country forcing weaker ones to agree by threatening force.
fact: Gunboat diplomacy describes bullying by powerful countries, so it is never a friendly way to make a deal.
exercise: «The gunboat sailed slowly up the river.» What is a gunboat? | *A small warship with big guns | A rowing boat for racing | A fishing boat with a big net | A huge cruise ship with a swimming pool
exercise: Where were gunboats often used? | Inside a castle moat | *On rivers and near the coast | High up on mountain tops | In the middle of the desert
exercise: «The strong country sent a gunboat to make the weaker country sign the deal.» What does gunboat diplomacy mean? | Friendly talks over a cup of tea | A race between the fastest boats on the sea | *A strong country forcing weaker ones to agree by threatening force | A school trip to see a river
test: «The crew watched the banks from the deck of the gunboat.» What does patrol mean? | To sleep in a tent | *To travel around an area to keep watch over it | To sell goods at a market | To race other boats
test: Why was gunboat diplomacy a form of bullying? | It let weaker countries choose freely | *It forced weaker countries to agree by threatening to use the navy or army | It made friends between countries | It was a kind way to share food
exam: Which word is closest in meaning to gunboat? | *Warship | Ferry | Rowboat | Lifeboat
